491c5","name":"Wang, Cancan","orcid":"0000-0002-9065-6884","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hanne Westh","key":"nicolajsenpau","last":"Nicolajsen","locid":"54728da8-274d-40e6-b119-23318d995721","name":"Nicolajsen, Hanne Westh","orcid":"0000-0002-0158-5034","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Cancan","key":"wangpedh","last":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cancan","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Laura","key":"junckerjensenpedh","last":"Juncker-Jensen","name":"Juncker-Jensen, Laura","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"edition":"1st","eisbn":["9788794538039"],"isbn":["9788794538039"],"pages":"1-13","place":"Copenhagen","status":"p","title":"Action Research Booklet"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d8b34e8e-2db9-456c-8d00-60e34b4b5fa2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Introduction: Action Research in a World of Crises and Care","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798561","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Deficient (d) DNA mismatch repair (MMR) is a biomarker predictive of better response to PD-1 blockade immunotherapy in solid tumors. dMMR can be caused by mutations in MMR genes or by protein inactivation, which can be detected by sequencing and immunohistochemistry, respectively. To investigate the role of dMMR in diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), MMR gene mutations and expression of MSH6, MSH2, MLH1, and PMS2 proteins were evaluated by targeted next-generation sequencing and immunohistochemistry in a large cohort of DLBCL patients treated with standard chemoimmunotherapy, and correlated with the tumor immune microenvironment characteristics quantified by fluorescent multiplex immunohistochemistry and gene-expression profiling. The results showed that genetic dMMR was infrequent in DLBCL and was significantly associated with increased cancer gene mutations and favorable immune microenvironment, but not prognostic impact. Phenotypic dMMR was also infrequent, and MMR proteins were commonly expressed in DLBCL. However, intratumor heterogeneity existed, and increased DLBCL cells with phenotypic dMMR correlated with significantly increased T cells and PD-1+ T cells, higher average nearest neighbor distance between T cells and PAX5+ cells, upregulated immune gene signatures, LE4 and LE7 ecotypes and their underlying Ecotyper-defined cell states, suggesting the possibility that increased T cells targeted only tumor cell subsets with dMMR. Only in patients with MYC¯ DLBCL, high MSH6/PMS2 expression showed significant adverse prognostic effects. This study shows the immunologic and prognostic effects of genetic/phenotypic dMMR in DLBCL, and raises a question on whether DLBCL-infiltrating PD-1+ T cells target only tumor subclones, relevant for the efficacy of PD-1 blockade immunotherapy in DLBCL.","author_count":31,"contributors":["Xu-Monette, Zijun Y","Luo, Cancan","Yu, Li","Li, Yong","Bhagat, Govind","Tzankov, Alexandar","Visco, Carlo","Fan, Xiangshan","Dybkær, Karen","Sakhdari, Ali","Wang, Nicholas T.","Yuan, Alyssa F.","Chiu, April","Tam, Wayne","Zu, Youli","Hsi, Eric D.","Perry, Anamarija M.","Song, Wenting","O'Malley, Dennis","Au, Qingyan","Nunns, Harry","Go, Heounjeong","Møller, Michael B.","Parsons, Benjamin M.","Montes-Moreno, Santiago","Ponzoni, Maurilio","Ferreri, Andrés J. M.","Sohani, Aliyah R.","Abramson, Jeremy S.","Xu, Bing","Young, Ken H."],"cuid":["S02-100.00-751531"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Young, Ken H."],"country":["Canada","Italy","South Korea","Spain","Switzerland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Asan Medical Center","Baylor College of Medicine","Columbia University Irving Medical Center","Columbia University Medical Center","Duke Cancer Institute","Duke University Medical Center","Gundersen Health System","Gundersen Lutheran Health System","Harvard Medical School","Harvard University","Houston Methodist","Houston Methodist Hospital","IRCCS Ospedale San Raffaele","Instituto de Investigación Sanitaria Valdecilla (IDIVAL)","Massachusetts General Hospital","Mayo Clinic","Mayo Clinic Rochester, MN","NeoGenomics Laboratories","New York Presbyterian Hospital","Northwell Health System","San Raffaele Scientific Institute","Translational Hematopathology Laboratory and Anatomic Pathology Service","Ulsan College","University Hospital Basel","University Hospital of Basel","University of Michigan","University of Michigan, Ann Arbor","University of Toronto","University of Verona"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Biomarkers Tumor genetics","DLBCL","DNA Binding Proteins genetics","DNA Mismatch Repair","DNA Mismatch Repair genetics","Female","Gene Expression Profiling","Genomic Instability","HDAC","Heterogeneity","Humans","Immune","Lymphoma Large B Cell Diffuse genetics","MSH6","MYC","Male","Mi