{"abstract":"EU’s KI-forordning stiller strenge krav til kunstig intelligens, men hæmmer ikke nødvendigvis udviklingen, sådan som erhvervslivet frygter. Kravene påvirker nemlig ikke kun europæiske virksomheder, men også globale aktører, der ønsker adgang til Europas indre marked.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Wang, Cancan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-764509"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["AI","EU","Investment","Regulation"],"level":"pop","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"virksom heder frygter ny ai lovgiv ning men glemmer vigtig ting companies fear new ai legislation but forget one important thing","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dna","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://borsen.dk/nyheder/opinion/virksomheder-frygter-ai-lovgivning-uden-grund","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-764509","ids":["itu-9f4e5679-7d34-4988-b623-6b5fd17d525f"],"keyword":["AI","EU","Investment","Regulation"],"lang":"English","level":"Popular","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-28D971358A22FFFEE71FEA9CA751DA26"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Digitalization, Democracy, and Governance, REFLACT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Cancan","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","locid":"605b59ab-91b4-4f8e-bb12-df68ce0491c5","name":"Wang, Cancan","orcid":"0000-0002-9065-6884","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["01050729","09068317"],"issn_org":["01050729"],"newspaper":"Børsen","status":"p","title":"Børsen"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9f4e5679-7d34-4988-b623-6b5fd17d525f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Virksom­heder frygter ny AI-lovgiv­ning, men glemmer vigtig ting : Companies fear new AI legislation, but forget one important thing","type":"Newspaper Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-764509","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Soil microorganisms and their diversity are important bioindicators of soil carbon and nutrient cycling. Land use type is a major determining factor that influences soil microbial community composition in floodplain ecosystems. However, how the structure and diversity of soil microbial communities respond to specific changes in land use, as well as the main drivers of these changes, are still unclear. This study was conducted in the Yellow River floodplain to examine the effects of land use type on soil microbial communities. Four land use types (shrubland, farmland, grassland and forest) were selected, wherein shrubland served as the baseline. We measured soil microbial structure and diversity using phospholipid fatty acids (PLFAs). Land use type significantly affected total, bacterial and fungal PLFAs, and the gram-positive/negative bacterial PLFAs. Compared with shrubland, peanut farmland had higher total and bacterial PLFAs and forest had higher fungal PLFAs. Soil pH and phosphorus were the predominate drivers of microbial PLFAs, explaining 37% and 26% of the variability, respectively. Soil total nitrogen and nitrate nitrogen were the main factors increasing microbial community diversity. Peanut farmland had the highest soil carbon content, soil carbon stock, total PLFAs and microbial diversity, suggesting that farmland has great potential as a carbon sink. Our findings indicated that peanut farmland in the Yellow River floodplain is critical for maintaining soil microbial communities and soil carbon sequestration.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Dong, Xiongde","Yang, Leyun","Harbo, Laura Sofie","Yan, Xinyu","Chen, Ji","Zhao, Cancan","Xiao, Yutong","Liu, Hao","Wang, Shilin","Miao, Yuan","Wang, Dong","Han, Shijie"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-668359"],"filters":{"added":"2023-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"corresponding_author":["Han, Shijie","Miao, Yuan","Wang, Dong"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Henan University","Henan University of Science and Technology","University of Chinese Academy of Sciences"],"keyword":["Bacteria","Fungi","Land use type","Soil carbon stock","Yellow River floodplain"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"effects of land use on soil microbial community structure and diversity in the yellow river floodplain","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/jpe/rtac075","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-668359","identifier":[{"id":"85152154314","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-8445e001-5e1e-461d-a082-d2b197c801f0"],"keyword":["Yellow River floodplain","bacteria","fungi","land use type","microbial community diversity","soil carbon stock"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-46C065D7962678C3BA8BA04FD41F424C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/jpe/rtac075","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/405156784/rtac075.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"henan university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Henan University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"henan university of science and technology","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Henan University of Science and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Agroecology, Department of Agroecology - Soil Fertility","Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, iCLIMATE Aarhus University Interdisciplinary Centre for Climate Change, iCLIMATE Aarhus University Interdisciplinary Centre for Climate Change - iCLIMATE Aarhus University Interdisciplinary Centre for Climate Change, Foulum"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"cn","key":"university of chinese academy of sciences","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"University of Chinese Academy of Sciences","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xiongde","key":"dongxiongdepau","last":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xiongde","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Leyun","key":"yangleyunpau","last":"Yang","name":"Yang, Leyun","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura Sofie","key":"harbolaurasofiepau","last":"Harbo","locid":"68dcbf71-515d-4b7f-85f9-c2ae5325fcba","name":"Harbo, Laura Sofie","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xinyu","key":"yanxinyupau","last":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xinyu","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ji","key":"chenjipau","last":"Chen","locid":"05f23b9e-16c0-478d-840b-18a8d914c393","name":"Chen, Ji","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Cancan","key":"zhaocancanpau","last":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Cancan","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Yutong","key":"xiaoyutongpau","last":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yutong","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hao","key":"liuhaopau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hao","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Shilin","key":"wangshilinpau","last":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shilin","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Yuan","key":"miaoyuanpau","last":"Miao","name":"Miao, Yuan","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dong","key":"wangdongpau","last":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dong","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Shijie","key":"hanshijiepau","last":"Han","name":"Han, Shijie","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/jpe/rtac075","issn":["1005264X","17529921"],"issn_org":["17529921"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of Plant Ecology","paperid":"rtac075","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"Journal of Plant Ecology","volume":"16"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85152154314"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-8445e001-5e1e-461d-a082-d2b197c801f0"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Effects of land use on soil microbial community structure and diversity in the Yellow River floodplain","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-668359","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a common inflammatory skin condition and prior genome-wide association studies (GWAS) have identified 71 associated loci. In the current study we conducted the largest AD GWAS to date (discovery N = 1,086,394, replication N = 3,604,027), combining previously reported cohorts with additional available data. We identified 81 loci (29 novel) in the European-only analysis (which all replicated in a separate European analysis) and 10 additional loci in the multi-ancestry analysis (3 novel). Eight variants from the multi-ancestry analysis replicated in at least one of the populations tested (European, Latino or African), while two may be specific to individuals of Japanese ancestry. AD loci showed enrichment for DNAse I hypersensitivity and eQTL associations in blood. At each locus we prioritised candidate genes by integrating multi-omic data. The implicated genes are predominantly in immune pathways of relevance to atopic inflammation and some offer drug repurposing opportunities.","author_count":132,"contributors":["Budu-Aggrey, Ashley","Kilanowski, Anna","Sobczyk, Maria K.","Shringarpure, Suyash S.","Mitchell, Ruth","Reis, Kadri","Reigo, Anu","Mägi, Reedik","Nelis, Mari","Tanaka, Nao","Brumpton, Ben M.","Thomas, Laurent F.","Sole-Navais, Pol","Flatley, Christopher","Espuela-Ortiz, Antonio","Herrera-Luis, Esther","Lominchar, Jesus V.T.","Bork-Jensen, Jette","Marenholz, Ingo","Arnau-Soler, Aleix","Jeong, Ayoung","Fawcett, Katherine A.","Baurecht, Hansjorg","Rodriguez, Elke","Alves, Alexessander Couto","Kumar, Ashish","Sleiman, Patrick M.","Chang, Xiao","Medina-Gomez, Carolina","Hu, Chen","Xu, Cheng jian","Qi, Cancan","El-Heis, Sarah","Titcombe, Philip","Antoun, Elie","Fadista, João","Wang, Carol A.","Thiering, Elisabeth","Wu, Baojun","Kress, Sara","Kothalawala, Dilini M.","Kadalayil, Latha","Duan, Jiasong","Zhang, Hongmei","Hadebe, Sabelo","Hoffmann, Thomas","Jorgenson, Eric","Choquet, Hélène","Risch, Neil","Njølstad, Pål","Andreass