{"abstract":"There are no cognitive studies of love conducted in an Asian culture. Our study is the first to probe the cognitive qualities that urban Chinese youth associate with what it means to be in love. To this end, we build on de Munck et al.’s pioneering studies in two European cultures (Russia, Lithuanian) and in the United States. Expanding on their study, we used a similar questionnaire that also includes additional questions designed to probe the youth of urban China’s perception and understanding of romantic love as an emotional and psychological force. We found that Chinese youths’ responses are similar to the Euro-American five core findings that all individuals, regardless of gender, experience when “in love.” These core attributes are as follows: “I will do anything for the person I love” (or altruism), “I constantly think about the person I am in love with” (or intrusive thinking), “romantic love is the supreme happiness of life” (or self-actualization), my “love makes my partner stronger and a better person,” (or emotional fulfillment), and “sexual attraction is necessary for love” (biology).","author_count":5,"contributors":["Jankowiak, William","Shen, Yifei","Yao, Shiyu","Wang, Cancan","Volsche, Shelly"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-279275"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["China","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Fudan University","University of Nevada Las Vegas"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"investigating love s universal attributes a research report from china","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-279275","identifier":[{"id":"84940937360","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-29150087-39fb-4920-94f6-ff374b18059e"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-561FE0C48CD50CF551BAE55B02649EBE"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of nevada las vegas","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Nevada Las Vegas","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"fudan university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Fudan University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of IT Management"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"William","key":"jankowiakpau","last":"Jankowiak","name":"Jankowiak, William","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Yifei","key":"shenpau","last":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yifei","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Shiyu","key":"yaopau","last":"Yao","name":"Yao, Shiyu","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Cancan","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","locid":"3e29e63c-70b6-4043-9f89-78f6de293337","name":"Wang, Cancan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Shelly","key":"volschepau","last":"Volsche","name":"Volsche, Shelly","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/1069397115594355","issn":["10693971","15523578"],"issn_org":["10693971"],"issue":"4","journal":"Cross-Cultural Research","pages":"422-436","publisher":"SAGE Publications Inc.","status":"p","title":"Cross-Cultural Research","volume":"49"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84940937360"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-29150087-39fb-4920-94f6-ff374b18059e"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Investigating Love’s Universal Attributes : A Research Report From China","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-279275","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"For a hundred years or more, The Nightingale and other fairytales by Hans Christian Andersen have fascinated readers in China and Denmark and bound them together in a corresponding set of values and preoccupations. Not only do the tales make use of poetic, ironic and humorous means to make a strong claim for the value of social justice, which continues to feature strongly in both regions, but throughout the 20th century the tales have also linked the vast empire ofthe east with the tiny kingdom of the north as imagined communities (Anderson1983). In H. C. Andersen’s eyes, China features as a refined land of porcelain, while Denmark is portrayed as a bucolic haven of peace. Beneath the polished surfaces, however,unruly and unjust social conditions prevail, and, according to H.C. Andersen, both societies stand in need of moral and ethical guidance. In both regions these tales have contributed to creating values and ethics for more than a century now, and the ugly duckling and the nightingale have remained well-loved figures, sending the message that there is an answer to the evils of unjust and societal hierarchies and a hope for social transformation.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Rømer Christensen, Hilda","Hauge, Bettina","Wang, Cancan"],"cuid":["S02-080.88-031793"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["hum","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"introduction gender dynamics and connecting comparisons","src":["cbs","dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/45295143/cancan_wang_gender_dynamics_publishersversion.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-080.88-031793","ids":["cbs-907d9aba-b173-496f-a35a-509e366bb82e","dtu-cd423a52-87a7-4ac3-8338-a9f13e57b41b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-2-0AB1B0F02B078F0EB308BFF0DEE697DA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/45295143/cancan_wang_gender_dynamics_publishersversion.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://tidsskrift.dk/index.php/KKF/article/viewFile/70204/127636","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/112407329/70204_140536_1_PB.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Management Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Management Engineering, Technology and Innovation Management"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of IT Management"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hilda","key":"rømerchristensenpau","last":"Rømer Christensen","name":"Rømer Christensen, Hilda","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hilda Rømer","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Hilda Rømer","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Bettina","key":"haugepau","last":"Hauge","locid":"2ca9805f-dbd5-41a9-bc4f-fd7774d2082b","name":"Hauge, Bettina","orcid":"0000-0002-4441-5975","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Cancan","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","locid":"3e29e63c-70b6-4043-9f89-78f6de293337","name":"Wang, Cancan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["09076182","22456937"],"issn_org":["09076182"],"issue":"1","journal":"Kvinder, Køn & Forskning","pages":"3-11","publisher":"Kobenhavns Universitet","status":"p","title":"Kvinder, Køn & Forskning","volume":"24"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-907d9aba-b173-496f-a35a-509e366bb82e","dtu-cd423a52-87a7-4ac3-8338-a9f13e57b41b"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School","DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Introduction: Gender Dynamics and Connecting Comparisons","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-080.88-031793","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"As the body of Information Systems (IS) research on social media grows, it faces increasing challenges of staying relevant to real world contexts. We analyze and contrast research on social media in the e-government field and in IS research, by reviewing and categorizing 63 studies published in key journal outlets, in order to identify and complement research foci and gaps. We find that, in comparison with egovernment social media research, IS studies tend to adopt an abstract view of the individual user, focus on a monetary view of value added by social media, and overlook the role of contextual factors. We thus propose an extended framework for mapping social media research, by including a focus on the role of context and environment, and identify a research agenda for future studies on social media-related phenomena relevant to real world contexts.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Wang, Cancan","Medaglia, Rony","Sæbø, Øystein"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-293517"],"event":{"end":"2015-12-12","place":"Fort Worth, us","start":"2015-12-12","title":"IFIP WG8.2 Organizations and Society in Information Systems (OASIS) 2015 Workshop","year":"2015"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Agder"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"learning from e government a framework and an agenda for social media research in is","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/58771472/Wang_Medaglia_S_b_OASIS_2015_FINAL.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-293517","ids":["